WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.860
Marcus names Elena as primary beneficiary and their adult daughter as

2
00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:10.360
contingent beneficiary. The primary beneficiary has first priority when

3
00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:11.800
eligible under the policy.

4
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:16.880
The contingent beneficiary takes when the primary cannot, according to the

5
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:22.000
stated succession rules. Naming an individual is different from designating

6
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.220
a class, such as my children.

7
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.700
Class wording must explain how shares pass if a member dies

8
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:34.700
first. Per capita generally divides among the surviving members

9
00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:35.840
of the stated class.

10
00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:41.400
Per stirpes preserves a deceased member's branch for eligible descendants.

11
00:00:42.620 --> 00:00:47.160
Read the actual designation instead of treating every child and grandchild

12
00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.720
as an equal recipient.

13
00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:52.550
Elena wants insurance money to support a young child.

14
00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:57.270
A minor generally cannot receive and manage a large claim as

15
00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:59.050
an independent adult payee.

16
00:00:59.710 --> 00:01:03.630
An appropriate trust or custodial arrangement can provide a legally

17
00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.910
authorized person to manage the funds.

18
00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:10.050
A trust beneficiary is different from naming the insured's estate.

19
00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:15.130
Proceeds payable to the estate enter the estate administration process.

20
00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:21.170
Proceeds payable directly to an eligible named beneficiary ordinarily pass

21
00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:25.830
outside probate. The policy designation, trust documents

22
00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:29.070
and applicable law must work together.

23
00:01:29.930 --> 00:01:32.850
Simply writing a different wish in a will does not ordinarily

24
00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.330
replace the policy's beneficiary form.

25
00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:39.260
A revocable beneficiary can ordinarily be changed through the owner's

26
00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:43.940
reserved contractual right. An irrevocable beneficiary has an interest that

27
00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:46.760
can restrict changes without the required consent.

28
00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:51.400
Those labels describe the ability to change rights, not whether the

29
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.080
person is primary or contingent.

30
00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:57.480
Maryland permits insurer acceptance or endorsement requirements before a

31
00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:58.880
change binds the insurer.

32
00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:01.920
Check assignments and other valid restrictions too.

33
00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:06.400
A producer should help complete and confirm the correct request, rather

34
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:09.300
than tell a client that a spoken instruction has already changed

35
00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:13.340
the insurer's records. Clear paperwork prevents avoidable disputes later.

36
00:02:13.974 --> 00:02:19.374
After a divorce or annulment, review the policy designation, governing

37
00:02:19.374 --> 00:02:23.554
law, court orders and any agreement affecting insurance.

38
00:02:24.434 --> 00:02:28.954
Do not assume that a family-status change automatically rewrites every

39
00:02:28.954 --> 00:02:33.974
contract. A recent Maryland Insurance Administration example required

40
00:02:33.974 --> 00:02:38.834
payment to a former spouse who remained the named beneficiary of

41
00:02:38.834 --> 00:02:40.214
a Maryland life policy.

42
00:02:40.694 --> 00:02:44.734
The insurer could not import another state's automatic divorce rule.

43
00:02:45.214 --> 00:02:48.854
Wills have their own Maryland revocation provision, which is a different

44
00:02:48.854 --> 00:02:53.654
document rule. Some federal programs or contracts also have special

45
00:02:53.654 --> 00:02:58.834
requirements. Identify the applicable arrangement before advising who

46
00:02:58.834 --> 00:02:59.914
will receive the money.

47
00:03:01.068 --> 00:03:05.168
Suppose an insured and the primary beneficiary die in the same

48
00:03:05.168 --> 00:03:09.168
accident. The order and timing of death can affect which recipient

49
00:03:09.168 --> 00:03:14.448
takes. Read any common-disaster clause or required survival period.

50
00:03:15.268 --> 00:03:19.988
In a teaching policy, the primary beneficiary must survive the insured

51
00:03:19.988 --> 00:03:24.148
by thirty days. The primary dies two days later.

52
00:03:24.968 --> 00:03:29.368
The contingent beneficiary remains alive and is eligible to receive payment.

53
00:03:30.108 --> 00:03:34.688
Under that stated clause, the primary fails the required period and

54
00:03:34.688 --> 00:03:39.788
the contingent takes. Thirty days is this example's contract term, not

55
00:03:39.788 --> 00:03:41.408
a universal Maryland requirement.

56
00:03:42.508 --> 00:03:46.488
The written lesson distinguishes Maryland's current rule from a change

57
00:03:46.488 --> 00:03:48.748
effective in October twenty twenty-six.

58
00:03:49.578 --> 00:03:53.838
A facility-of-payment provision can permit the insurer to pay an eligible

59
00:03:53.838 --> 00:03:58.998
person under specified circumstances when ordinary beneficiary payment cannot

60
00:03:58.998 --> 00:04:03.638
proceed as expected. Maryland's individual-life statute describes situations

61
00:04:03.638 --> 00:04:08.058
such as a minor beneficiary, a beneficiary who died first,

62
00:04:08.898 --> 00:04:10.358
or the other listed conditions.

63
00:04:11.158 --> 00:04:15.400
It identifies permitted recipients and limits the provision's operation.

64
00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:19.500
It does not let an insurer ignore a valid adult beneficiary

65
00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:22.198
merely because another person asks for the money.

66
00:04:22.458 --> 00:04:26.278
Distinguish this provision from an owner's beneficiary change and from a

67
00:04:26.278 --> 00:04:30.778
policy assignment. Start with the actual clause, the claimant's status

68
00:04:31.458 --> 00:04:32.818
and the applicable legal conditions.
